WEBVTT

00:05.005 --> 00:08.675
Good... You can't run anymore...

00:14.305 --> 00:16.891
<i>Before Stanley's crew
chases us for the last time</i>

00:17.684 --> 00:19.436
<i>in their fighter jets...</i>

00:22.021 --> 00:25.859
We'll make it
if we just run through the mountains!

00:25.942 --> 00:27.235
Yes.

00:27.318 --> 00:28.737
The Amazon is famous

00:29.654 --> 00:33.199
<i>for its ridiculously dense trees
that act as a natural barrier.</i>

00:33.283 --> 00:36.995
<i>They are our trump card
against the fighter jets.</i>

00:37.787 --> 00:39.330
But beyond the mountains,

00:39.414 --> 00:42.709
the jungle really
seems to stretch on forever.

00:42.792 --> 00:44.586
Yep, there's plenty of it!

00:44.669 --> 00:45.712
Which means...

00:46.880 --> 00:51.885
It's time to craft
our 3D maneuvering gear!

00:52.469 --> 00:54.304
A ropeway!

00:56.681 --> 00:58.141
ORIGINAL WORK: RIICHIRO INAGAKI, BOICHI
SHUEISHA "JUMP COMICS"

02:32.318 --> 02:33.820
Still...

02:34.404 --> 02:35.989
This looks insanely deep!

02:36.573 --> 02:40.160
Even with a ropeway,
climbing this would be impossible.

02:41.536 --> 02:44.330
Guess this is where I come in.

02:52.797 --> 02:54.299
How is this even possible?

02:55.550 --> 02:57.385
You better not forget.

02:57.969 --> 03:00.972
<i>I've been training for years,
climbing every day</i>

03:01.055 --> 03:03.725
<i>to fetch hot spring water
for my sister Ruri.</i>

03:04.392 --> 03:07.103
Climbing on my own is no hassle, but...

03:08.187 --> 03:10.899
as expected, I can't carry the motorbikes
and everyone else.

03:10.982 --> 03:12.942
Now, what?

03:24.787 --> 03:27.081
The ropeway's going to use this thin rope?

03:27.165 --> 03:31.377
No, that's just the guide.
Now we're going to reel in the wire.

03:31.461 --> 03:32.420
Huh?

03:33.004 --> 03:36.132
But Kohaku doesn't have
a cell phone with her.

03:36.216 --> 03:38.134
How are we supposed
to give her instructions?

03:38.218 --> 03:41.429
No way, the enemy
is coming in a plane, right?

03:41.512 --> 03:44.974
If we send out any radio waves,
we'll give away our position too.

03:46.059 --> 03:48.895
There's a rope set up,
so we should be able to go wired.

03:48.978 --> 03:50.355
- Wired?
- Wired?

03:52.315 --> 03:54.567
Can you hear me, lioness--

03:54.651 --> 03:55.693
Stop it!

03:56.277 --> 03:57.487
A string phone!

03:57.570 --> 04:00.031
Whoa! This feels nostalgic!

04:00.114 --> 04:03.117
Going low-tech is handy
in moments like this.

04:18.925 --> 04:21.678
YOU FOUND A ROPEWAY!

04:22.470 --> 04:25.348
Sliding a pulley along a stretched cable.

04:26.349 --> 04:28.017
It's a simple mechanism.

04:28.726 --> 04:31.896
<i>It's the same type of overhead cable
used in forestry and other industries.</i>

04:32.397 --> 04:35.149
Yes... the only danger is the weight.

04:35.233 --> 04:39.570
If me, Kohaku, and Tsukasa,
the Power Team, goes up first,

04:39.654 --> 04:42.824
we can easily pull up around 100kg!

04:42.907 --> 04:44.951
Why use human power?

04:45.034 --> 04:48.538
We've got engines and tires.
We'll wind it up with machines.

04:49.163 --> 04:51.916
The problem is the cable won't hold up.

04:52.417 --> 04:54.669
A wire of carbon and iron.

04:54.752 --> 04:57.755
It's light and durable.
It's actually elegant.

04:58.715 --> 05:02.510
<i>The maximum load capacity
of the Science Kingdom's ropeway is...</i>

05:03.219 --> 05:06.347
roughly 160kg.

05:06.431 --> 05:09.434
Then we could split up our things
to carry them.

05:09.517 --> 05:11.894
We humans should go in pairs.

05:11.978 --> 05:15.023
It should be fine
if we just base the pairs on our weights.

05:16.441 --> 05:18.568
Good thing
I calculated everything precisely!

05:18.651 --> 05:20.236
<i>Pairs, huh?</i>

05:20.320 --> 05:22.947
<i>Relaying on numbers alone
could be dangerous.</i>

05:23.031 --> 05:24.032
Huh?

05:24.782 --> 05:26.200
Xeno is the problem.

05:26.784 --> 05:29.412
<i>Say we choose randomly, for instance.</i>

05:29.495 --> 05:32.332
<i>What would happen if Xeno and Suika
ended up being the last ones?</i>

05:38.629 --> 05:42.383
Looks like we're in a situation similar
to a "crossing the river" riddle.

05:42.467 --> 05:44.260
A "crossing the river" riddle?

05:45.094 --> 05:46.721
<i>Let's say you're a traveler</i>

05:46.804 --> 05:49.932
<i>who wants to cross the river
with your luggage.</i>

05:50.016 --> 05:54.604
<i>Your luggage consists of three items.
A fox, a chicken and a corncob.</i>

05:54.687 --> 05:57.482
<i>You can only take one item
with you on the boat at once.</i>

05:58.274 --> 06:02.361
<i>If you take the corncob first,
leaving the fox and the chicken alone,</i>

06:02.945 --> 06:05.281
<i>the fox eats the chicken.</i>

06:05.907 --> 06:09.660
<i>If you take the fox,
leaving the chicken and the corncob...</i>

06:10.328 --> 06:12.997
<i>The chicken eats the corncob!</i>

06:13.581 --> 06:17.502
<i>So, how can you take all your luggage
with you safely?</i>

06:17.585 --> 06:19.128
It's a famous riddle.

06:19.212 --> 06:22.215
If you don't know the answer,
try to solve it ahead!

06:23.049 --> 06:26.719
My biggest concern
is the pair formed by Hyoga and Xeno.

06:27.553 --> 06:28.554
First of all, Hyoga...

06:29.305 --> 06:32.600
<i>is the man
who killed Tsukasa for his ideals.</i>

06:33.351 --> 06:36.229
Personally, I'm not sure
about what he's thinking now.

06:36.979 --> 06:38.898
If he ends up alone with Xeno...

06:40.149 --> 06:41.526
I don't even want to think about it.

06:42.110 --> 06:45.947
<i>You and I are basically the same, Xeno.</i>

06:46.030 --> 06:49.784
<i>Let's make a kingdom
just for the chosen ones.</i>

06:49.867 --> 06:54.080
<i>The two of us can now
run away and join Stanley's crew.</i>

06:54.163 --> 06:57.291
<i>Whoa, that's actually
an elegant suggestion...</i>

06:58.209 --> 07:01.337
Wait, wait! Hyoga killed Tsukasa?

07:01.921 --> 07:03.923
No way! But you all get on so well!

07:04.006 --> 07:05.800
Human relationships are weird!

07:05.883 --> 07:09.846
Right, I get it's hard to explain
from your perspective, Chelsea.

07:09.929 --> 07:13.724
Let me briefly explain
everyone's relationship diagram.

07:13.808 --> 07:14.642
CRAFT TEAM - BATTLE TEAM
CHILDHOOD FRIENDS

07:14.725 --> 07:17.353
<i>Hyoga killed Tsukasa.</i>

07:17.437 --> 07:19.856
<i>Tsukasa killed Senku.</i>

07:19.939 --> 07:22.275
<i>Senku killed Tsukasa.</i>

07:22.358 --> 07:24.777
<i>Xeno killed Senku.</i>

07:24.861 --> 07:26.612
That's way too many "killed"!

07:26.696 --> 07:29.157
When you put it that way...

07:30.324 --> 07:33.327
In the end, the problem is Xeno, right?

07:33.411 --> 07:36.330
If only one of them climbs
while holding the prisoner,

07:36.414 --> 07:39.834
it has to be someone from the Battle Team.

07:41.085 --> 07:42.753
It's barely doable.

07:42.837 --> 07:45.423
Any one of them
would exceed the 160kg weight limit.

07:46.090 --> 07:48.301
So I have to go down once again?

07:48.384 --> 07:51.387
<i>This really looks like
the "crossing the river" riddle.</i>

07:51.471 --> 07:54.432
Hey, but Xeno and Hyoga can ride together!

07:54.515 --> 07:58.186
We just said
it'd be a terrible idea to pair those two!

07:59.437 --> 08:01.105
It's fine, isn't it?

08:01.189 --> 08:04.442
Never mind the details,
we need to move fast!

08:12.325 --> 08:14.327
Yahoo!

08:24.212 --> 08:25.963
Awesome!

08:46.234 --> 08:48.611
We're almost done...

08:53.658 --> 08:54.784
Suika!

09:09.757 --> 09:11.342
This is bad! That was close!

09:11.425 --> 09:15.346
Luckily, Kohaku saved her
with one of her trademark jumps.

09:17.515 --> 09:18.558
<i>Wait...</i>

09:18.641 --> 09:22.728
<i>Right now, there's no one
on this side who can fight!</i>

09:22.812 --> 09:26.774
<i>Yes. Right now,
Hyoga could betray us without any risk.</i>

09:27.358 --> 09:31.362
<i>Since I'm protecting Suika,
I can't defend myself.</i>

09:31.445 --> 09:33.114
<i>If he pushes me into the abyss...</i>

09:52.174 --> 09:53.175
Huh?

09:55.177 --> 09:58.931
You're a nice guy after all, Hyoga!
I like you so much!

09:59.640 --> 10:02.059
Even if you're not all friends
on the relationship diagram,

10:02.143 --> 10:04.645
you really get along well!
This is funsies!

10:05.563 --> 10:06.689
Yes.

10:06.772 --> 10:08.733
I guess we've been through a lot...

10:22.038 --> 10:24.707
We witnessed the beginning
of the petrification...

10:25.249 --> 10:26.667
The Science Kingdom...

10:27.251 --> 10:28.336
which is us...

10:29.920 --> 10:31.047
are now...

10:32.006 --> 10:33.758
a single team!

10:37.637 --> 10:41.057
I really hope
there's a huge natural barrier

10:41.641 --> 10:45.728
that spreads beyond the mountains...

10:51.359 --> 10:54.904
Hey, we finally did it.

10:56.447 --> 10:59.075
We reached the Amazon jungle!

10:59.158 --> 11:01.202
Our trump card!

11:12.880 --> 11:17.301
We've reached the ultra-dense
South American Jungle Crossing course!

11:19.637 --> 11:21.680
Once we're in the Amazon,

11:21.764 --> 11:25.226
there's nothing to fear
from Stanley's fighter jets!

11:27.812 --> 11:29.814
It looks like they lost us.

11:29.897 --> 11:30.731
Yes.

11:30.815 --> 11:34.819
Shooting them down from the sky
in the desert would've been way too easy.

11:35.611 --> 11:39.407
The first half ends with a narrow victory
for the Science Club kids!

11:39.990 --> 11:41.325
That's enough for us.

11:41.409 --> 11:44.036
We've found
our objective of flying in here

11:44.120 --> 11:46.372
in the form of motorcycle tracks.

11:47.665 --> 11:52.002
The Science Club kids' route of advance
has been identified.

11:53.462 --> 11:57.341
It might be another trick,
the way the motorbikes are moving.

11:57.425 --> 11:59.135
That's why we provoked them.

11:59.218 --> 12:02.054
To make sure they'd flee straight ahead.

12:02.763 --> 12:04.807
To bait them, we said on the radio

12:04.890 --> 12:06.809
that we were repairing
and modifying the plane.

12:08.227 --> 12:13.357
I'm sure that Captain Stanley
is playing a game he's sure to win.

12:14.900 --> 12:19.280
I know which escape route
the enemies are taking.

12:28.998 --> 12:31.333
All right, we lost them!

12:31.417 --> 12:34.837
Let's get to our goal,
the source of humanity's petrification!

12:34.920 --> 12:36.338
That's Manaus.

12:36.422 --> 12:39.216
<i>A place east of here in Brazil.</i>

12:39.300 --> 12:40.426
CURRENT LOCATION
BRAZIL - SOUTH AMERICA

12:40.509 --> 12:44.096
We just need to keep going straight ahead!

12:44.763 --> 12:49.185
Well, I suppose that's the way to go,
but that would mean crossing the Amazon.

12:49.768 --> 12:54.440
Yes, it's not a place
for weak human bodies to traverse.

12:54.523 --> 12:56.609
I'm ten billion percent sure we could die.

12:56.692 --> 12:58.486
Ten billion percent?

12:59.487 --> 13:01.947
Beneath our feet are hellish insects.

13:02.031 --> 13:04.742
parasitic sand fleas,
blood-sucking stink bugs,

13:04.825 --> 13:07.495
and giant leeches,
any one of which could kill us.

13:09.121 --> 13:12.249
That's the purpose
of human wisdom and science.

13:12.333 --> 13:14.627
To strengthen our fragile bodies.

13:14.710 --> 13:15.878
RUBBER BOOTS ACQUIRED!

13:15.961 --> 13:18.088
There are so many uses for rubber!

13:21.342 --> 13:25.513
The water splashes and pops,
but none of it's getting in.

13:26.514 --> 13:28.933
These are really good!

13:35.189 --> 13:38.025
- Can we eat this too?
- Huh?

13:38.817 --> 13:40.152
Whoa!

13:40.236 --> 13:42.655
That's huge!

13:43.614 --> 13:45.157
It's closely related to a jackfruit.

13:45.908 --> 13:49.370
<i>It's known as the biggest fruit on Earth.</i>

13:49.453 --> 13:54.166
<i>Fully ripened ones have a refreshing,
mild sweetness and are delicious.</i>

13:55.334 --> 13:56.252
Oh...

13:57.628 --> 13:58.921
Huh?

13:59.964 --> 14:02.132
That's impressive, Master Gen.

14:02.216 --> 14:04.677
This is awful!

14:27.283 --> 14:29.326
What are you burning, Xeno?

14:29.410 --> 14:33.581
I'm making a mist to repel insects
using Umbelliferae and Artemisia plants.

14:34.123 --> 14:36.375
Mosquitoes in the Amazon are fierce.

14:36.458 --> 14:39.336
I don't want to get bitten
when you're caught up in this.

14:39.837 --> 14:41.964
There are bugs everywhere...

14:43.841 --> 14:47.261
What do you think is the strongest
creature living in the Amazon right now?

14:47.344 --> 14:48.178
Huh?

14:48.262 --> 14:50.264
It's Tsukasa!

14:50.347 --> 14:53.183
Well, that's not entirely wrong...

14:53.267 --> 14:56.228
No, I can't compete with that, either.

14:56.312 --> 14:57.146
Huh?

14:57.229 --> 15:01.400
The king of the Amazon,
the strongest creature alive on Earth

15:02.026 --> 15:03.861
is the army ant.

15:03.944 --> 15:05.195
Yikes!

15:05.779 --> 15:08.407
I think I've seen it in a movie!

15:08.490 --> 15:10.618
Army ants attack in swarms,

15:10.701 --> 15:12.828
and they can even
strip great beasts to the bone!

15:13.621 --> 15:15.122
Yes!

15:15.205 --> 15:16.290
How could you!

15:20.586 --> 15:22.004
Sorry for the trap.

15:22.087 --> 15:23.923
She's harvesting them!

15:24.006 --> 15:26.258
No way!

15:28.802 --> 15:31.430
It's kind of like that snake sake.

15:31.513 --> 15:34.725
I made alcohol using an army ant extract.

15:34.808 --> 15:37.978
I can smell a hint of dashi soup.

15:38.062 --> 15:40.731
Army ants smell like bonito broth!

15:40.814 --> 15:42.983
It's hilarious and funsies!

15:43.609 --> 15:45.611
This powerful extract...

15:45.694 --> 15:47.613
- No way!
- You're...

15:53.744 --> 15:55.079
Coat yourself with it.

15:56.580 --> 15:58.123
I see!

15:58.207 --> 15:59.875
It repels the strongest creature,

15:59.959 --> 16:02.836
so even the Amazon's monsters
won't get near us.

16:02.920 --> 16:05.005
I heard this a long time ago.

16:05.089 --> 16:06.882
It's the wisdom of the locals.

16:06.966 --> 16:10.344
Field work is great, right, Chelsea?

16:10.427 --> 16:13.389
Phew, I thought we were going to drink it.

16:13.472 --> 16:14.974
This is not as bad.

16:15.057 --> 16:18.227
Bugs... over... my skin?

16:19.478 --> 16:21.480
- My lady!
- My lady!

16:41.375 --> 16:42.626
Here it is...

16:43.669 --> 16:45.295
The Amazon River!

16:46.046 --> 16:47.798
Awesome!

16:47.881 --> 16:49.717
Yahoo!

16:53.303 --> 16:55.055
Whoa!

16:59.643 --> 17:01.645
It's like a water slide!

17:02.312 --> 17:03.480
Ha!

17:06.942 --> 17:09.945
The final sprint is to keep
following the river all the way

17:10.029 --> 17:12.031
down to the hypocenter, Manaus.

17:12.614 --> 17:14.324
It's got to be there!

17:14.408 --> 17:17.703
The first petrification device
that turned everyone on Earth to stone.

17:19.121 --> 17:21.498
It's been such a great adventure so far.

17:21.582 --> 17:23.417
We can't go home empty-handed!

17:23.500 --> 17:26.253
We can only hope to find our treasure!

17:27.588 --> 17:31.133
But looking for it
is going to be hellish work.

17:32.134 --> 17:35.304
The Medusa thing is so small.

17:38.057 --> 17:42.644
Hey, Chrome.
Are you busy building something?

17:42.728 --> 17:45.230
No, I was thinking
about this powerful extract.

17:45.314 --> 17:49.318
I was trying to make something that sprays
the stuff automatically with a time delay.

17:52.362 --> 17:54.698
<i>A time delay...</i>

17:54.782 --> 17:57.910
<i>That's it! The beam from the Medusa too...</i>

18:03.707 --> 18:06.376
Senku! I just had a crazy idea!

18:06.460 --> 18:08.545
The machine that guards
the petrification beam...

18:09.463 --> 18:12.132
Senku, you succeeded in Treasure Island,
didn't you?

18:12.216 --> 18:15.886
You developed a technique to use the
revival fluid right after petrification.

18:15.969 --> 18:18.180
We'll automate that!

18:18.263 --> 18:20.307
Something that splashes it
with a time delay!

18:22.351 --> 18:24.853
If you were Whyman,

18:24.937 --> 18:28.315
how would you make sure
to wipe out humanity for good?

18:28.398 --> 18:31.568
No tricks, no misfires,
no chance of escape...

18:33.654 --> 18:37.116
Firing multiple petrification beams
in rapid succession.

18:37.908 --> 18:39.576
That is... a multi-stage attack.

18:39.660 --> 18:41.203
Huh...

18:41.286 --> 18:43.914
The light from thousands of years ago
was the same...

18:43.997 --> 18:48.085
Does that mean there's more
than one Medusa at ground zero?

18:50.546 --> 18:52.214
Who knows?

18:52.297 --> 18:56.343
Anyway, we're finally doing
an on-site inspection of the crime scene.

18:57.010 --> 18:58.887
This is tempting...

19:02.349 --> 19:04.726
JANUARY, AD 5742

19:04.810 --> 19:08.063
WEDNESDAY, THURSDAY, FRIDAY, SATURDAY

19:24.329 --> 19:25.747
Over here...

19:33.714 --> 19:35.966
<i>Four months after we left Japan,</i>

19:36.049 --> 19:39.761
<i>we have arrived
at the opposite point on Earth.</i>

19:43.599 --> 19:46.143
<i>To the origin
of that terrible petrification light</i>

19:46.810 --> 19:49.229
<i>from thousands of years ago.</i>

20:01.366 --> 20:07.289
If Whyman really did throw in
several Medusas with outright hostility,

20:07.873 --> 20:10.834
truly determined to wipe out
humanity for good...

20:10.918 --> 20:11.835
Huh?

20:21.678 --> 20:23.680
There's one petrification device here!

20:23.764 --> 20:26.183
Are you for real?

20:26.266 --> 20:28.685
Awesome! We're really lucky!

20:29.394 --> 20:30.479
"Lucky"?

20:31.521 --> 20:35.192
This doesn't seem like just that...

20:58.548 --> 21:02.302
This is a sickening, yet beautiful...

21:03.262 --> 21:04.304
menace.

22:38.440 --> 22:40.901
This is the solution
to the "crossing the river" riddle.

22:40.984 --> 22:41.985
Yes!

22:43.111 --> 22:44.613
<i>Let's say you're a traveler</i>

22:44.696 --> 22:47.574
<i>who wants to cross the river
with your luggage.</i>

22:47.657 --> 22:49.326
<i>Your luggage consists of three items.</i>

22:49.409 --> 22:51.620
<i>A fox, a chicken and a corncob.</i>

22:51.703 --> 22:54.706
<i>You can only take one item
with you on the boat at once.</i>

22:55.582 --> 22:58.335
<i>But if you leave the fox
and the chicken alone,</i>

22:58.418 --> 23:00.670
<i>the fox will eat the chicken.</i>

23:01.463 --> 23:04.341
<i>And if you leave
the chicken with the corncob,</i>

23:04.424 --> 23:06.510
<i>the chicken eats the corncob.</i>

23:07.219 --> 23:11.139
<i>First, the traveler crosses the river
with the chicken on board.</i>

23:11.681 --> 23:14.101
I don't eat corn.

23:17.187 --> 23:18.146
<i>Then he gets back</i>

23:18.230 --> 23:20.065
<i>and crosses the river
with the fox on board.</i>

23:22.484 --> 23:24.236
<i>Then he takes back the chicken</i>

23:25.237 --> 23:27.322
<i>and crosses with the corncob on board.</i>

23:29.825 --> 23:32.077
I'm not scared of the fox.

23:33.370 --> 23:36.581
<i>Then, he gets back and crosses
with the chicken on board.</i>

23:40.043 --> 23:42.921
<i>This safely finishes the crossing!</i>

23:44.214 --> 23:45.215
<i>The end!</i>

23:53.014 --> 23:55.892
NEXT EPISODE

23:55.976 --> 23:58.145
THIS WORK OF FICTION FEATURES ORGANISMS,
MATERIAL AND PROCESSES BASED ON REAL LIFE.

23:58.228 --> 23:59.980
COLLECTING OR REPLICATING THEM
MAY BE DANGEROUS AND ILLEGAL,

24:00.063 --> 24:00.897
SO DO NOT ATTEMPT.

24:00.981 --> 24:01.815
Subtitle translation by: Silvia Tomey
